    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `AcceptanceTest` \u2014 1 run, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\nOnly one file \u2014 `SaveOwner.java`, the create-success step in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline. It already runs at the point where the owner has been persisted (id assigned) and its `memberId` (`customerCode`) is finalized, and it already emits the `AUDIT` log lines there. I added:\n\n1. A `NOTIFY` logger field: `LoggerFactory.getLogger(\"NOTIFY\")`.\n2. One line at the end of `service(...)`:\n   ```java\n   notify.info(\"welcome owner id={} memberId={}\", owner.getId(), memberId);\n   ```\n\nThe test (`AuditLogCapture(\"NOTIFY\")` \u2192 `anyContains(id, memberId)`) sees a line containing both, e.g. `welcome owner id=11 memberId=NSW2716E6609E1`.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n`SaveOwner` is a single-method class, so `WMC_other = 0` (\u2192 clamped to 1) and its `service` method has cyclomatic complexity 1. The added behaviour is one statement (one changed line in the function), reusing the already-computed `owner.getId()` and `memberId`. The cost is essentially `1 \u00d7 1 \u00d7 1` over a single touched file \u2014 the file-count multiplier stays at 1. Spinning up a separate function class + a YAML pipeline step would have doubled the touched-file multiplier for no metric benefit, since the notify point is exactly where the id and memberId already exist.",
